  "rel.beta_h": "Sensitivity beta (arm length)",
  "rel.bn": "{v}bn",
  "rel.bn_per1": "{v}bn USD per 1%",
  "rel.cap_beta": "<b>This figure</b>: how many % each index moves when yields rise 10bp.<br><b>How to read</b>: the larger the (absolute) value, the more rate-sensitive.<br><b>Now</b>: Nasdaq100 {ndx}% / Dow30 {dow}% / S&P500 {spx}%. Nasdaq is the most sensitive because its earnings lie further in the future.<br><b>Source / method</b>: <span class=\"k\">daily log returns of each index × daily DGS10 change (bp), OLS slope over the last {days} days. FRED.</span><br><b>Assumptions / limits</b>: <span class=\"k\">Dow being more sensitive than the S&P500 is the opposite of the textbook; kept as an observation, not a conclusion.</span>",
  "rel.cap_cond": "<b>This figure</b>: thermometers for loose/tight financial conditions and recession.<br><b>How to read</b>: NFCI/ANFCI negative = loose; 10Y-3M negative = inverted curve (recession signal); Sahm triggers at 0.5.<br><b>Now</b>: NFCI {nfci} / 10Y-3M {t10y3m} / Sahm {sahm}.<br><b>Source / method</b>: <span class=\"k\">FRED. Each series carries 5-year percentile context (%tile / z / d20z).</span><br><b>Assumptions / limits</b>: <span class=\"k\">US only. For weekly/monthly series d20 means 20 weeks/months.</span>",
  "rel.cap_credit": "<b>This figure</b>: corporate bond spreads (a thermometer of credit stress).<br><b>How to read</b>: higher = more credit stress.<br><b>Now</b>: HY {hy} / IG {ig}.<br><b>Source / method</b>: <span class=\"k\">FRED BAMLH0A0HYM2 / BAMLC0A0CM.</span><br><b>Assumptions / limits</b>: <span class=\"k\">US only.</span>",
  "rel.cap_decomp": "<b>This figure</b>: the breakdown of the identity nominal yield = real yield + expected inflation.<br><b>How to read</b>: the same yield affects equities in opposite ways depending on whether the real part or expected inflation rose.<br><b>Now</b>: nominal {nom}% = real {real}% + expected inflation {be}% (check error {gap}). Of {exnom}%, {tp}% is the term premium (risk charge) and the remaining <b>{exp}%</b> is the expected future policy rate.<br><b>Source / method</b>: <span class=\"k\">FRED DGS10 / DFII10 / T10YIE / THREEFYTP10. Nominal − term premium = expectations.</span><br><b>Assumptions / limits</b>: <span class=\"k\">US only. EU/JP have no free daily real-yield or term-premium series.</span>",
  "rel.cap_dte": "<b>This figure</b>: intraday amplification from same-day expiries (0DTE).<br><b>How to read</b>: open-interest-based total GEX underestimates 0DTE.<br><b>Now</b>: <b>unavailable</b> in this feed (the prior-close snapshot no longer contains 0DTE; contracts {n}).<br><b>Source / method</b>: <span class=\"k\">CBOE delayed feed. Capturing 0DTE needs a real-time feed, which is not available for free.</span><br><b>Assumptions / limits</b>: <span class=\"k\">Not added to gex_oi. Shown as unavailable, not 0.</span>",
  "rel.cap_gamma": "<b>This figure</b>: how option dealers react to price moves (the walls below are gamma by strike).<br><b>How to read</b>: negative total GEX = dealers sell into falls and buy into rallies, amplifying moves; positive = moves are damped.<br><b>Now</b>: {gex}bn USD per 1%, zero gamma {zg}, spot {spot} is {zone}.<br><b>Source / method</b>: <span class=\"k\">CBOE public option chain (SPX, {n} contracts with open interest). {snap}.</span><br><b>Assumptions / limits</b>: <span class=\"k\">{note} Coverage: US only (EU/JP unavailable because JPX/Eurex do not publish Greeks).</span>",
  "rel.cap_netliq": "<b>This figure</b>: the amount of money circulating in markets (net liquidity).<br><b>How to read</b>: the larger, the more of a tailwind for risk assets such as equities.<br><b>Now</b>: primary gauge, bank reserves (WRESBAL) <b>{p} T USD</b>. Reference shortcut formula (WALCL−TGA−RRP) {ref} T USD.<br><b>Source / method</b>: <span class=\"k\">FRED. RRP is nearly exhausted ({rrp} T USD) and carries little information, so WRESBAL is promoted to the primary gauge.</span><br><b>Assumptions / limits</b>: <span class=\"k\">The shortcut formula is a social-media reference value; WRESBAL is the primary measure.</span>",
  "rel.cap_regime": "<b>This figure</b>: shows whether stocks and bonds move in opposite directions.<br><b>How to read</b>: negative = stocks fall when yields rise (the balance works); positive = stocks and bonds fall together (the balance breaks).<br><b>Now</b>: r = <b>{r}</b> ({label}). <span class=\"k\">History: covid {covid} / 2022 {y2022} / last 1y {y1}.</span><br><b>Source / method</b>: <span class=\"k\">daily log returns of equities × daily yield changes (bp), 120-day rolling. Changes, not levels (levels show spurious correlation from trends). n={n}.</span><br><b>Assumptions / limits</b>: <span class=\"k\">The 60/40 premise depends on this sign. Computed for the US (SP500 × DGS10).</span>",
  "rel.cap_region": "<b>This figure</b>: {region} equity (blue solid) overlaid with {lines}.<br><b>How to read</b>: when yields rise, bonds become relatively attractive and money tends to leave equities (downward pressure).<br><b>Now</b>: {now}.<br><b>Source / method</b>: <span class=\"k\">equity {eqsrc}, yields {src}. Matched on common business days (inner join, no forward fill).</span><br><b>Assumptions / limits</b>: <span class=\"k\">{limit}</span>",
  "rel.cap_volstruct": "<b>This figure</b>: the term structure of fear, the tail, and the rates-vol ratio.<br><b>How to read</b>: VIX3M/VIX&gt;1 = normal (contango), &lt;1 = stress (backwardation). A high MOVE/VIX means rates volatility is leading.<br><b>Now</b>: ratio {ratio} ({state}), SKEW {skew}, MOVE/VIX {mv}.<br><b>Source / method</b>: <span class=\"k\">CBOE VIX/VIX3M/SKEW CSV; MOVE from data.json.</span><br><b>Assumptions / limits</b>: <span class=\"k\">US only.</span>",
  "rel.cap_walls": "<b>This figure</b>: the price “walls” where moves tend to stall or amplify.<br><b>How to read</b>: positive (green) = pinning, negative (red) = amplifying.<br><b>Now</b>: largest wall {strike} ({gex}).<br><b>Source / method</b>: <span class=\"k\">CBOE gamma × open interest, aggregated in 25-point buckets.</span><br><b>Assumptions / limits</b>: <span class=\"k\">Depends on the conventional assumption about who is long/short (below).</span>",
  "rel.cond_h": "Financial conditions and the cycle (NFCI / ANFCI / StL Stress / dollar index / 10Y-3M / Sahm)",
